(window.__CLAIMS_ADD__||function(){})([{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F1: FTC Consumer Sentinel 2025: 3 million fraud reports and $15.9 billion in REPORTED losses (the FTC's June 2026 release rounds it to \"about $16 billion\", the highest on record, up about 25% on 2024). 2024 was 2.6 million reports and $12.5 billion. Series: over $6B (2021), nearly $9B (2022), over $10B (2023), over $12B (2024), $15.9B (2025).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a1"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F2: The FTC's own MODELED estimate of real fraud losses in 2025 is $38.1B to $261.5B for all ages and $13.3B to $119.5B for people 60+, depending on assumptions about underreporting; its 2024 high-end all-ages estimate was $195.9B. A study the FTC cites found 4.8% of mass-market fraud victims complained to a BBB or government body.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a2"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F3: Investment scams were the top fraud by reported dollars in 2025: over $7.9B, about half of all reported losses, average loss over $10,000. Imposter scams were the most-reported fraud (every year since 2020): over 1 million reports, over $3.5B. Business impersonators took nearly $1B, with the highest losses to bank impersonators; government impersonators about $920M.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a3"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F4: 2025, all ages: text was the most-reported contact method, but social media led by reported dollars ($2.1B, about 8x 2020's $261M; $1.1B of it investment scams, $298M romance). Facebook led platforms, WhatsApp and Instagram a distant second and third. Share of LOSS reports by contact method: website/app 31%, social 28%, phone 11%, email 10%, text 7%. People 60+: median loss $3,000 when it began with a phone call vs $599 on social media.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a4"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F5: How the money leaves: bank transfers/payments carry the most reported dollars, then cryptocurrency; credit cards are named most often but carry little. FTC dollar totals by payment method DO exist: all ages 2024 (Data Book) and people 60+ 2025 (Figure 7); no medians by payment method were found.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a5"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F6: Gift cards (Dec 2021 FTC spotlight, first nine months of 2021): nearly 40,000 people reported $148M stolen through gift cards; scammers named the brand and the store (Target most often), kept victims on the phone and coached them for the cashier; Target cards had the highest median loss, $2,500. Newer figures: $212M on 41,120 reports (all ages, 2024); $139M for people 60+ in 2025.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a6"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F7: Crypto ATMs: FTC reported losses rose nearly tenfold to over $110M in 2023 and topped $65M in the first half of 2024 (median $10,000); people 60+ were more than three times as likely to report losing money this way. IC3 2025 (separate dataset): 13,460 kiosk complaints, $389M, of which people 60+ $257.5M (about two-thirds). FinCEN issued a notice to banks on Aug 4, 2025. Iowa AG: over $20M from Iowans in under three years, mostly victims over 60; Bitcoin Depot takes 23%, CoinFlip \"claims\" 21%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a7"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F8: People 60+ reported over $3B in fraud losses in 2025 (about $2.4B in 2024; about $600M in 2020), across about 123,000 loss reports. Median loss about $900 for all 60+ (held steady) and $2,000 for 80+ (up 21%, highest of any group). Six-figure losses were 6% of older adults' loss reports but 70% of their dollars. Older adults were 15% more likely to report losing money to a friend/family impersonation scam.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a8"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F9: Older Americans are NOT the most likely to lose money: in 2025, population-adjusted, people 60+ reported losing money at a 14% lower rate than 18–59s and filed no-loss reports at a 34% higher rate; 74% of their reports showed no loss. In 2021, 18–59s were 34% more likely to report a loss. When older people do lose, the median loss is higher and rises with age.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a9"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F10: FBI IC3 2025 (separate dataset): 1,008,597 complaints, $20.877B reported losses (up 26%), average $20,699. Investment $8.65B; IC3 separately counts crypto investment fraud at $7.228B (61,559 complaints). Tech/customer support $2.13B (from $1.46B). Government impersonation $797.9M on 32,424 complaints (2024: $405.6M, 17,367). People 60+: 201,266 complaints, about $7.7B (up 59%), average $38,500; 60+ took $1.04B (49%) of tech-support losses and 52% ($413.2M) of government-impersonation losses on 27% of its complaints.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a10"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F26: Industry survey totals are not counted data: Truecaller/Harris says 21% of US adults (56.2M) lost $25.4B to phone scams in 2023, from about 2,000 respondents; the total is the survey's $452 average loss multiplied up to the adult population. GASA/Feedzai says $1.03 trillion lost globally (58,329 respondents), US average $3,520 per victim. Both are vendors in the anti-scam business.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a11"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U1: IC3 2025, people 60+: 201,266 complaints (up 37%), $7.748B losses (up 59%), average $38,500; 12,444 lost more than $100K. Age is optional; complaints without one are left out of age tables.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a12"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U2: IC3 2025 crypto ATM/kiosk: 13,460 complaints, $389M (complaints up 23%, losses up 58%); people 60+: 6,188 complaints, $257.5M (about 66%).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a13"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U3: FTC: $470M reported lost to scams that began with a text in 2024, more than five times 2020; the share of text reports with a loss rose from 5% (2020) to 11% (2024). Fake package-delivery texts (usually posing as USPS) were the most reported; unpaid-toll texts were in the top five.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a14"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U5: IC3 2025: crypto investment fraud was the largest source of losses, $7.2B (61,559 complaints); scammers typically make contact by text, social media, ads or dating apps, then move the chat to a messaging platform.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a15"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U6: FTC 2025: $7.9B to investment scams out of $15.9B; social media $2.1B, of which $1.1B investment (more than half); about 8x 2020's $261M; nearly 60% of people who lost money to romance scams said it began on social media; text was 7% of loss reports with a contact method.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a16"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U7: IC3 government impersonation: 14,190 complaints/$394.1M (2023), 17,367/$405.6M (2024), 32,424/$797.9M (2025): both roughly doubled in 2025.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a17"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U8: IC3 tech/customer support: 47,794 complaints and $2.13B in 2025 (36,002 and $1.46B in 2024; $0.92B in 2023). People 60+: 21,333 complaints, $1.04B (about half); second-most-reported 60+ crime type after phishing/spoofing (48,064).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a18"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U9: IC3 government impersonation, 60+: 8,628 of 32,424 complaints (27%) but $413.2M of $797.9M (52%), up from $208.1M in 2024. Employment: 60+ lost $78.7M of $362.9M (22%), up from $37.9M.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a19"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U10: FTC testimony (March 2026): imposter scams most reported since 2020 (over 1M reports, over $3.5B in 2025); investment top by dollars (over $7.9B, about half); text top contact method by reports, social media top by dollars (over $2B, stated as FY 2025); bank payments then crypto top by dollars, credit card most frequent; $15.9B total; series over $6B/nearly $9B/over $10B/over $12B for 2021–2024.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a20"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U11: FTC older-adults report (Dec 2025, 2024 data): 60+ reported losses nearly $2.4B in 2024, from about $600M in 2020; 80+ median over $1,600; top 60+ loss types investment, then business impersonation (largest part bank impersonators, often telling people to put cash in Bitcoin ATMs to keep it \"safe\"), then government impersonation (up 47%); 60+ five times (400%) more likely to report a tech-support loss ($159M); job-scam losses up nearly 300% on task scams; by phone, 40% of 60+ loss REPORTS were business impersonation, 23% government.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a21"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U13: FTC report to Congress (Oct 2026): about $16B reported lost in 2025; 60+ over $3B (from about $2.4B); 80+ median $2,000; older adults 15% more likely to report a friend/family impersonation loss.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a22"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U19: In 2021 adults 18–59 were 34% more likely than 60+ to report losing money to fraud (population-adjusted), while older people who lost had higher medians ($800 for 70–79, $1,500 for 80+).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a23"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U20: FTC counted baseline: $15.9B reported in 2025 on 3 million reports, up from over $12B in 2024.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a24"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U21: The FTC says reported losses are a fraction of real ones (4.8% of mass-market fraud victims complained to a BBB or government body); its underreporting-adjusted 2024 estimate runs up to $195.9B, about 16 times the $12.5B reported for 2024 (not 12 times).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a25"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U22: FTC 2025: 3M reports, $15.9B; bank payments then crypto top by dollars, credit cards most often named; text top contact method by reports, social media top by dollars (over $2B, FY 2025). The testimony itself gives no dollar split by method (other FTC documents do).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a26"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U35: 2025 (now confirmed in the primary, not only the ppc.land summary): 60+ lost money at a 14% lower rate and filed no-loss reports at a 34% higher rate than 18–59s; 74% of their reports showed no loss; 60+ median about $900, 80+ $2,000 (up 21%); 60+ reported losses over $3B.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a27"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U45: Truecaller/Harris (vendor survey): 21% of US adults (56.2M) lost $25.4B to phone scams in 2023, from about 2,000 adults surveyed in January 2024; its 2022 edition said 26% and $39.5B. The FTC counted over $10B for ALL fraud in 2023, so the survey's phone-only total is more than double it (different method, not like-for-like).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a28"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U48: FTC gift-card spotlight (Dec 2021): first nine months of 2021, nearly 40,000 people reported $148M stolen via gift cards; scammers named brand and store (Target most often), kept victims on the phone, coached them on what to tell cashiers; Target cards had the highest median loss, $2,500.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a29"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U49: Investment scams top by dollars (over $7.9B, average loss over $10,000, about half of 2025 losses); imposter scams most reported (over 1M reports, over $3.5B).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a30"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U50: 2025: bank payments highest aggregate losses, then crypto; credit cards most frequently identified; text top reported contact method, social media top by losses (over $2B, FY 2025).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a31"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U51: The 31/28/11/10/7 figures are each contact method's SHARE OF LOSS REPORTS in 2025 (website/app 31%, social 28%, phone 11%, email 10%, text 7%), not the share of each method's reports that showed a loss. Social media losses $2.1B ($1.1B investment, $298M romance); Facebook most, then WhatsApp and Instagram.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a32"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U52: Method behind the FTC's range: HIGH assumes Sentinel captures 2.0% of losses under $1,000 and 6.7% of losses of $1,000+ (from nine FTC cases matched to Sentinel); LOW assumes everyone losing $10,000+ reported. Age assumed missing at random within loss brackets (77% of losses had age; 46% of those were 60+). The Oct 1–Nov 13, 2025 lapse was filled with the mean of the 30 days before and after.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a33"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U53: Older adults 2025: losses over $100,000 were 6% of loss reports but 70% of dollars; median $3,000 for phone-initiated fraud vs $599 for social media; for 80+ the phone produced the most losses and loss reports; older adults' text-scam losses up 61%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a34"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U54: IC3: crypto investment fraud is the largest source of loss ($7.2B), largely run by Southeast Asian organized crime using trafficked forced labor; contact by text, social media, ads or dating apps, then moved to another platform. \"Cryptocurrency\" is a descriptor (any reference to virtual currency) that applies across crime types.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a35"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U55: IC3 2025: 181,565 crypto-related complaints and $11.366B (complaints up 21%); people 60+ about $4.4B on more than 40,000 complaints. IC3 prints two different 60+ figures (44,555 / $4.43B in the crypto section; 42,271 / $4.35B in the elder section), so round it.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a36"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U56: IC3 2025: 22,364 complaints with AI-related information, adjusted losses over $893M; a descriptor across fraud types, not a scam type.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a37"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U61: FTC reported-loss series: over $6B (2021), nearly $9B (2022), over $10B (2023), over $12B (2024), $15.9B (2025); the rise is driven largely by more reports of losses of $100,000 or more (in 2025, 3% of loss reporters lost $100K+ but that was $11B, about 69% of reported dollars).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a38"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U69: FTC report to Congress (Oct 2026): 60+ reported over $3B lost in 2025; 80+ median $2,000; older adults (60+) lost more in aggregate to social-media-initiated fraud than any other contact method (80+ is the exception: phone); real older-adult losses estimated up to $119.5B.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a39"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U74: The loss-rate gap by age holds over years: 2017, 40% of fraud reporters in their 20s reported a loss vs 18% of those 70+ (CNBC on the FTC data book); 2024 (FTC Data Book, primary), 44% of 20–29s vs 24% of 70–79s and 21% of 80+.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a40"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U78: GASA/Feedzai 2024 (vendor survey): $1.03 trillion lost globally in 12 months, from 58,329 respondents; US average loss per victim $3,520. Cannot be tied to counted data.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a41"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U80: Hiya \"86% of unknown calls go unanswered\" (2026, 12,000 consumers): NOT supported by the cited page, which reports Hiya's FIRST State of the Phone Call report (11 billion calls): 76% of calls from unknown numbers unanswered and about 9% of spam calls answered. Do not use 86% until Hiya's own 2026 report is opened.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-a42"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U99: IC3 method differs from Sentinel: complaints filed with IC3; analysts apply crime type and \"adjusted loss\"; statistics are a point-in-time assessment; one crime type per complaint; losses de-duplicated as much as possible; age optional. Never add IC3 to Sentinel (Sentinel also ingests part of IC3's data).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a43"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F24a: On Nov 5, 2026, OFAC sanctioned 4 individuals and 41 entities that support the Prince Group network, plus one Malaysian individual (Chui Kok Wa): 46 designations in total.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F24b: Legal authority: 45 targets were designated under E.O. 13581 (transnational criminal organizations) and one, Chui Kok Wa, under E.O. 13694 (malicious cyber-enabled activity). Treasury calls the action \"in furtherance of\" E.O. 14390 (Mar 6, 2026), but E.O. 14390 is not the designation authority.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b2"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F24c: E.O. 14390 is \"Combating Cybercrime, Fraud, and Predatory Schemes Against American Citizens\", signed Mar 6, 2026 and published Mar 11, 2026.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b3"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F24d / U24: Chui Kok Wa led a Madagascar-based network of scammers that targeted Americans and defrauded them of over $400,000 equivalent, including digital assets.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b4"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F24e: What the release says about American victims: Treasury Secretary Bessent says scam syndicates \"continue to steal billions of dollars from American victims\". The release describes digital-asset investment scams, romance scams and other online fraud. It does not mention text messages, phone calls or SMS.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b5"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U23: The 46 targets are offshore companies, nominee/administrative facilitators and family members helping Prince Group executives evade the Oct 14, 2025 and June 23, 2026 sanctions.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b6"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U37a: Oct 14, 2025: OFAC sanctioned 146 targets in the Prince Group network.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b7"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U37b: The same day, DOJ unsealed an indictment of Prince Group chairman Chen Zhi (wire fraud conspiracy, money laundering conspiracy) and filed a civil forfeiture complaint against about 127,271 bitcoin, worth about $15B at the time. Forfeiture was sought in a complaint, not ordered. Chen's reported arrest in Cambodia and transfer to China were not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b8"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F23a / U27a: FCC order FCC 26-67 (\"Enhancing the Ability of Consumers To Control the Robocalls\"), adopted Sept 30, 2026, published in the Federal Register Oct 26, 2026 (91 FR 67214, doc 2026-21821), effective Nov 25, 2026.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b9"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F23b: Bank fraud alerts: banks could already send certain fraud-alert calls and texts to cell phones without prior consent, but only to a number the customer gave the bank. From Nov 25, they may also use a number from a \"reliable source\": one supplied by a spouse or family member authorized on the account, one captured when the customer calls the bank, or one in records from another financial institution. Banks must still honor opt-outs.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b10"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F23c: Revoking consent: a caller may now name ONE exclusive way to opt out: a key-press/voice opt-out on the call, a reply text with a standard word (\"stop,\" \"quit,\" \"end,\" \"revoke,\" \"opt out,\" \"cancel,\" \"unsubscribe\"), or a website/phone number it discloses. If it names one, it need not process opt-outs made any other way.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b11"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F23d: Is \"reply STOP\" enough? It works when the sender has designated reply-text as its method (then any of the seven words must be honored). If the sender instead designates a website or phone number as the exclusive method, a STOP reply need not be processed. A sender using a no-reply texting protocol must say so in each text and give other ways to revoke. Opt-outs must be honored within 10 business days.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b12"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F23e: Category-only revocation: an opt-out sent in reply to an informational message (no marketing) may be treated as covering only that category, e.g. stopping payment reminders without stopping fraud alerts. The FCC concedes some consumers will have to opt out more than once. An opt-out from a telemarketing message still covers all telemarketing from that caller.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b13"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F11a: Crypto investment fraud was the largest loss line in IC3's 2025 report: $7.2B reported. IC3 says it is largely run by Southeast Asian organized crime using trafficked people as forced labor.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b14"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F11b: IC3's pattern: first contact by text, social media, ads or dating apps; the chat moves to a messaging app; fake platform, fake profits, loans to invest more, then \"taxes and fees\" to withdraw, then recovery scams. IC3 does not count \"wrong number\" texts separately.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b15"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F11c: The FTC names \"wrong number\" texts as a top-5 text scam of 2024: a fake friendship, then a pitch for a bogus investment platform. No count or dollar figure is given for this type.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b16"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F12a: People reported $470M lost to scams that started with a text in 2024, more than five times the 2020 figure.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b17"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F12b: Only 11% of 2024 text-scam reports said money was lost (5% in 2020).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b18"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F12c: Fake package-delivery texts (usually posing as USPS) were the most reported text scam of 2024. Unpaid-toll texts ranked fourth. The top five were estimated from a hand-coded sample of 1,000 reports and make up about half of text frauds.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b19"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F12d / U4: IC3 PSA (April 12, 2024): over 2,000 complaints since early March 2024 about toll-debt smishing texts from at least three states. The link impersonates the state toll service. The PSA advises securing accounts and disputing charges, and does not say what data is stolen.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b20"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F12e: FTC 2025 data: older adults' no-loss text reports were often about texts impersonating state motor-vehicle offices and toll-road operators. This is the only 2025 primary that mentions tolls, and it gives no dollar figure.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b21"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U95: Mechanism (secondary, Krebs, Apr 2025): the fake toll/USPS site asks for card data, then for a one-time code. The bank actually sent that code because the phishers were adding the victim's card to a mobile wallet on their own device.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b22"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U70: Krebs, citing vendors: about 25,000 Smishing Triad phishing domains active in any 8-day period (SilentPush); about a 5% average success rate and one site capturing 30 card records from 550 interactions in 7 days (Prodaft). These are vendor figures.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b23"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U29: Google sued the operators of the Lighthouse smishing kit in Nov 2025. Per Google (reported by Infosecurity), the kit targeted over 1 million people in 121+ countries and offered 600+ phishing templates.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b24"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U30: Google estimates 12M–115M payment cards stolen in the US through Lighthouse, a very wide range from the plaintiff.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b25"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U103: Within days of the suit, Google told SecurityWeek that Lighthouse was disrupted; the operators told customers their cloud server was shut down. The group has been active since at least 2023. The general counsel's \"win\" quote was not checked.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b26"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U31: SMS blasters are portable fake base stations (backpack or car) that push texts to nearby phones and bypass carrier filtering. The arrests cited are abroad; no US case appears in the article.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b27"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U67: Secret Service, Sept 2025: about 300 SIM servers and 100,000 SIM cards seized in the NY tri-state area, framed as a threat to officials tied to nation-state actors, not as a consumer-scam case. The \"30 million texts a minute\" figure was not found in the saved copy.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b28"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F13a / U12: IC3 2025: victims claimed over $5M in losses to \"distress\" (grandparent) scams. The figure sits in IC3's AI section under Confidence/Romance scams \"with a likely AI-nexus\". Read it as the AI-linked slice, not all grandparent scams. It is the only grandparent figure in the report.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b29"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F13b: IC3 2025 counted 22,364 complaints with AI-related information and adjusted losses over $893M, across all crime types (investment-scam complaints with an AI nexus alone passed $632M). This is not a voice-clone count.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b30"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U101: IC3 describes distress scams as using voice cloning to mimic a loved one, and says the scam is spreading to other family members and friends.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b31"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U57: FBI PSA I-120324-PSA (Dec 3, 2024): criminals generate short audio clips of a loved one's voice to fake a crisis; the FBI advises a family secret word or phrase. It is guidance with no counts.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b32"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U46: No FTC or IC3 count of confirmed voice-clone cases exists. The \"1 in 4\" figure is McAfee's 2023 online survey of 7,054 adults in seven countries, self-reported and vendor-run.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b33"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F14a / U102: The Do Not Call Registry is a list for law-abiding telemarketers. It does not block calls and will not stop scammers.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b34"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F14b / U96: \"Can you hear me?\": in 2017 the FCC did warn that the scam records a \"yes\" as a \"voice signature\". The ABC report on that warning names no victim and no charge; the one caller interviewed saw nothing happen.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b35"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U97: Snopes (now read in full, 2017) found no scenario where a bare recorded \"yes\" could authorize charges without the scammer already holding much of the victim's account information. Interviewees reported the calls, not losses.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b36"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U73: The \"46 complaints\" are from Sen. Collins's office in 2017 (Apr 2–May 16): complaints about the calls, with no loss or charge described.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b37"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F14c / U98: Silent calls / \"hello\" cloning: ITRC (an advocacy nonprofit, Mar 2026 podcast citing ZDNET) says silent calls mostly check that a number is live, and a quick \"hello\" is usually not enough to clone a voice. No primary source shows a \"hello\"-based clone.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b38"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F14e / U36a: Crypto \"never recoverable\" is overstated, but recovery means restraint or seizure, not refunds. DOJ's Scam Center Strike Force said in Apr 2026 it had restrained over $700M in scam-linked crypto, while still \"working tirelessly to forfeit funds\" for victims.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b39"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U36b: The earlier Strike Force milestone was over $580M in crypto frozen or seized (Feb 26, 2026).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b40"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U36c: IC3's Recovery Asset Team (Financial Fraud Kill Chain), 2025: 3,900 incidents, $1.16B attempted theft, $679M frozen, a 58% success rate. This replaces the engine's 2024 figures. It works on wires and bank transfers caught fast, not crypto already sent.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b41"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U36d: The June 2025 civil forfeiture complaint against $225.3M in crypto: DOJ page blocked (401), Cointelegraph blocked.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-b42"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U100: IC3: the DC US Attorney's Scam Center Strike Force (with DOJ Criminal Division, FBI, Secret Service; partnering with State, OFAC, Commerce) targets Southeast Asian compounds and their leaders, and seizes US-based infrastructure. Operation Level Up (launched Jan 2024) notifies crypto-fraud victims: 8,935 notified as of Mar 2026, 77% unaware they were being scammed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b43"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F22a / U81: The FCC itself says the STIR/SHAKEN caller-ID signature is lost whenever any part of a call's path is non-IP. It proposed on Apr 28, 2025 to close that gap.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b44"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U64: Whether a final non-IP authentication order was adopted after the Apr 2025 proposal: not found.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-b45"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F22b / U65: Rule effective Sept 18, 2025: third parties may sign calls, but all calls must be signed with the certificate of the provider holding the STIR/SHAKEN obligation.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b46"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F22c / U66: Aug 25, 2025: the FCC removed over 1,200 non-compliant voice providers from the Robocall Mitigation Database, cutting them off from US networks, after removing a first 185 earlier that month.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b47"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U94: 2020 Second Report and Order: a continuing STIR/SHAKEN extension for non-IP network portions, conditioned on upgrading to IP or working on a non-IP solution. Seen only via a law-firm summary.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b48"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F25a / U39: UNODC (July 21, 2026): scam losses across East Asia, South-East Asia, Australia and New Zealand are estimated at $88.3B–$114.1B for 2025. \"About triple two years earlier\" and \"displaced, not dismantled\" are NOT in UNODC's press release; they come from news coverage.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b49"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F25b / U40: The 120,000 (Myanmar) and 100,000 (Cambodia) figures are OHCHR's Aug 2023 estimate of people who \"may be held\" and forced to run scams. They are not from UNODC's April 2025 report.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b50"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F25c / U79: Amnesty International (June 26, 2025) identified at least 53 scam compounds in Cambodia with abuses, and called the government's response woefully ineffective, pointing towards complicity. The 58-survivor and 20-site details were not seen on the page.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b51"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F25d / U38: Displacement (AP via The Star, Dec 2025): the KK Park raid scattered thousands of workers to other compounds. C4ADS found 14 of 21 known Myawaddy compounds had shown construction or expansion since January.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b52"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U75: SpaceX said it cut off over 2,500 Starlink units in Myanmar, and AP found at least two compounds still online by Starlink afterward. The figures \"30 terminals seized\" and \"2,000+ released\" were not checked (Arab News behind a CAPTCHA).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b53"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U41: Thailand's Feb 2025 power, internet and fuel cuts to Myanmar border hubs: the source page did not load.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-b54"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F25e / U62: FinCEN's Section 311 final rule (announced Oct 14, 2025; published Oct 16; effective Nov 17, 2025) cuts Huione Group off from US correspondent accounts. FinCEN found Huione received at least $4B in illicit proceeds from Aug 2021 to Jan 2025. Cambodia's central bank rescinded Huione Pay's license per a July 31, 2025 update, not in January 2025.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b55"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U32: FinCEN notice (Aug 4, 2025): in 2024 IC3 received more than 10,956 crypto-kiosk complaints with about $246.7M in losses, up 99% and 31% from 2023. The \"two-thirds of losses by people 60+\" share was not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b56"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U28: Iowa AG (Feb 2025) sued Bitcoin Depot and CoinFlip under the Iowa Consumer Fraud Act. Hundreds of Iowans sent more than $20M through their ATMs in under 3 years, most scam victims over 60. Bitcoin Depot takes a 23% cut and CoinFlip claims 21%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b57"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U44: DC AG (Sept 2025) alleges 93% of Athena Bitcoin kiosk deposits were the direct result of scams, and fees of up to 26% per transaction. The \"first five months\" window in the release attaches to a different figure (48% of funds reported to Athena as scams). These are allegations.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b58"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U26: Australia (ACCC/NASC): 2025 scam losses were A$2.18B, up 7.8% on 2024 and down 29.7% from the 2022 peak of A$3.1B.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b59"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"S1: Per-scam (tech support): IC3 2025 had 47,794 tech-support complaints, with $2.13B lost.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b60"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"S2: Per-scam (government impersonation): IC3 2025 had 32,424 complaints and $797.9M lost. FTC 2025 losses by impersonated agency: $161M (FTC), $144M (SSA), $28M (FBI).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b61"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"S3: Per-scam (bank impersonation): IC3 2025 \"Account Takeover\" (linked to its PSA on impersonation of financial-institution support): about 4,700 complaints, $359.7M. FTC: among business impersonators, banks drew the highest losses from older adults (no dollar figure given).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b62"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"S4: Per-scam (job/task): IC3 2025 Employment: 24,688 complaints, $362.9M. FTC: job-scam losses topped $220M in H1 2024, driven by task scams that usually start with an unexpected text or WhatsApp message.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b63"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"S5: Per-scam (call-centre total): IC3 says tech/customer support plus government impersonation totaled over 80,000 complaints and over $2.9B in 2025.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b64"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"S6: FTC: the government impersonation figures by agency (any age, 2025).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b65"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"S7: FTC task-scam figure.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b66"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"S8: FTC \"false alarm\" spotlight (Aug 2025): from 2020 to 2024, reports from older adults who lost $10K+ to business or government impersonators rose more than fourfold. Of those, 41% started with a phone call.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b67"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F15: Not answering unknown calls and not tapping links: no study in the record, and none found in this pass, measures whether this reduces losses. The Hiya answer-rate figures (86% of unknown calls unanswered; 11% of labelled spam answered) come from a vendor survey reported second-hand and were NOT re-checked here. Studies of the closest thing, mass awareness messaging, find little effect (see G3).","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-c1"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F16: The FBI recommends a family secret word and hanging up to call the organization back on a number you look up yourself. The FTC says resist pressure and talk to someone you trust. Neither page cites any effectiveness data, and this pass found no study that measures code words or call-backs. The only nearby outcome evidence is on warnings and education (G1, G2).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c2"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F17: Google's 70% is fewer SPAM calls for Pixel users with Call Screen, compared with users who have no Call Screen features, from Google's internal data on Pixel 6 and newer. The 2018 Mind Commerce carrier figures (93.6/90.1/86.9%) came from test calls spoofed from invalid or unallocated numbers. The Lionbridge \"14%\" is NOT in the cited RCR article, so it is unresolved. The Leviathan comparison was commissioned by Google and publishes no rates.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c3"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F18: A credit freeze is free by federal law. It must be placed within 1 business day of a phone or online request and lifted within 1 hour (3 business days by mail). Children under 16 count as \"protected consumers\". The law took effect 120 days after 24 May 2018, which is 21 Sep 2018. A freeze stops new credit being opened in your name. It does not stop misuse of existing accounts or a payment you send, and it must be placed at all three bureaus.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c4"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F19: Reg E protects only UNAUTHORIZED transfers. A transfer the scammer makes with login details you were tricked into sharing IS unauthorized (CFPB FAQ). A payment you send yourself is not. Wires are excluded from Reg E and fall under UCC 4A, where a payment cannot be cancelled after acceptance unless the receiving bank agrees. FedNow money must be paid out \"immediately\". The CFPB's Zelle suit was dismissed with prejudice in March 2025. PSI's 38% (2023, down from 62% in 2019) is the reimbursement rate on FRAUD (unauthorized) disputes at JPMorgan, BofA and Wells: $64M of $166M. SCAM disputes were almost never reimbursed. Zelle's June 2023 imposter rule reached about 15–20% of scam-disputed transactions, $18.3M.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c5"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F20: UK figures are as stated in the dossier: 126,000 claims, 88% of money claimed returned (up from 66%), 84% resolved within five business days, £112M. The independent Frontier Economics evaluation found the value of in-scope Faster Payments APP scams about 21% lower per month, about £73m a year. It is a theory-based evaluation, not an experiment, and out-of-scope international APP losses rose £39m. Singapore: the bank pays first, then the telco, then the consumer (phishing only). Australia: A$2.18B in 2025, down 29.7% from the A$3.1B peak in 2022 but UP 7.8% on 2024.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c6"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"F21: First hour: call your bank and ask for a recall, then file at ic3.gov whatever the amount. The RAT Kill Chain froze $679.0M of $1.164B attempted in 3,900 incidents in 2025 (58%). In 2024 it froze $561.6M ($469.1M domestic plus $92.5M international) of $848.4M across 3,020 complaints (66%). In 2023 it held $538.39M of $758.05M across 3,008 incidents (71%). The success rate fell from 71% to 66% to 58%. Victims 60+ in 2025: 642 incidents, $32.87M frozen of $65.37M. The by-payment steps are as stated (FTC).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c7"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U14: Google says Pixel users with Call Screen had 70% fewer spam calls on average. This is internal data, compared with users with no Call Screen features. It is not a scam-detection rate.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c8"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U15: The CFPB sued EWS, BofA, JPMorgan Chase and Wells Fargo on 20 Dec 2024 under the CFPA (unfairness) and EFTA/Reg E. It voluntarily dismissed with prejudice on 4 Mar 2025, and the court dismissed on 5 Mar 2025. No court ruled on the merits.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c9"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U16: PSI (July 2024): the three banks reimbursed about 38% of FRAUD (unauthorized) disputes in 2023, $64M of $166M, down from 62% in 2019. Separately, about $207M was disputed as SCAMS at the three banks in 2023, and scam disputes were rarely reimbursed (JPMorgan reimbursed 3 of 41,390 scam disputes in 2020). The \"$372M disputed / $270M unreimbursed\" pair is not in the report. It roughly matches $166M + $207M, but the press release is blocked (403) and could not be checked.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c10"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U17: FBI PSA I-120324-PSA recommends a family secret word or phrase and hanging up to call back on a number you research yourself. The page cites no effectiveness data.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c11"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U18: 2025 Kill Chain: 3,900 incidents (3,574 domestic, 326 international), $1,163,919,846 attempted theft, $679,013,183 frozen, 58%. The rate covers only incidents RAT acted on.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c12"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U25: PSR, first nine months (Oct 2024 to Jun 2025): 126,000 claims (about 15% fewer than a year earlier), 88% of money claimed returned (from 66%), 84% resolved within five days, 97% within 35 days, £112M reimbursed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c13"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U33: A secondary summary of the FCC's 2020 staff report puts false blocks at 0.2–0.3% of analysed calls (provider estimates). The FCC primary was not read, so \"no participation counts or effectiveness rates\" is unverified.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c14"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U34: Zelle network banks began reversing transfers as of 30 June 2023 for customers tricked into paying scammers posing as a government agency, a bank or an existing service provider. EWS withheld the details of the policy. The \"not carte blanche\" quote is NOT in the saved NBC article, so drop it. This is a private network rule, not law.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c15"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U42: The independent evaluation (Frontier Economics, report dated 26 Jun 2026, published by the PSR in July 2026) found the value of APP scams over Faster Payments about 21% lower per month than the 2023 pre-policy average, about £73m a year. It covers in-scope scams in the first year only.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c16"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U43: Singapore's SRF (from 16 Dec 2024) uses a waterfall: the FI pays if it breached its duties, then the telco, then the consumer. It covers only phishing where credentials are revealed on a fake digital platform and lead to unauthorised transactions.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c17"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U47: The $50 and $500 tiers apply when a lost or stolen ACCESS DEVICE (card, code) is involved. The two-business-day clock runs from when you LEARN of the loss or theft. For unauthorized transfers without an access device, you owe nothing if you report within 60 days of the statement. \"Unlimited\" applies only to transfers after that 60-day window. All of this covers unauthorized transfers only.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c18"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U58: Free freezes took effect 21 Sep 2018 (120 days after the 24 May 2018 law). Placement takes 1 business day by phone or online and lifting takes 1 hour. Under-16s can be frozen free. The FTC's 2018 press-release URL now returns \"Page not found\"; the statute was used instead.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c19"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U59: 2025 RAT results for victims 60+: 642 incidents, $65,367,648 reported loss, $32,865,655 frozen (about 50%). The top types were Tech Support/ATO (360), BEC (104) and Investment (64). ATO incidents can involve 50+ simultaneous ACH transfers.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c20"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U60: RAT was established in February 2018. In 2023 it ran the Kill Chain on 3,008 incidents with $758.05M potential loss and placed holds on $538.39M (71%).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c21"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U63: PSR survey: 71% of UK APP fraud victims were unaware of the reimbursement policy and 49% did not try to get reimbursed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c22"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U68: The CFPB's suit alleged that customers of the three banks lost more than $870M over Zelle's seven years. It was dropped in March 2025. (The $870M is the CFPB's allegation, reported second-hand.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c23"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U71: Mind Commerce (2018) reported identification accuracy of 93.6% (Verizon), 90.1% (T-Mobile) and 86.9% (AT&T) on test calls spoofed from invalid or unallocated numbers. The Lionbridge \"14% of scam calls\" figure is NOT in this source (unresolved). Both predate today's tools.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c24"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U72: PSI: Zelle's June 2023 policy produced $18.3M in reimbursed scam claims in six months. EWS itself estimated that about 15–20% of scam-disputed transactions qualified. This is a share of disputes, NOT \"under 20% of victims\". The Checkbook page would not download (21 bytes), so the primary was used.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c25"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U76: Meta says WhatsApp took down 6.8M accounts \"linked to criminal scam centers\" in the first six months of 2025. The article's example is a Cambodian scam center. \"Mostly in Southeast Asia\" and the March 2026 150,000-account Thai operation are NOT in this source (unresolved).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c26"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U77: Leviathan's October 2025 assessment of the Pixel 10 Pro, iPhone 17 Pro, Galaxy Z Fold 7 and Razr+ 2025 was commissioned by Google and ran on T-Mobile for about a month. The page gives no block or false-positive rates.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c27"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U82: Reg E protects only transfers initiated by someone other than the consumer without actual authority. A payment the victim sends herself is not covered. The CFPB's \"fraudulently induced\" FAQ is about a scammer who tricks you into SHARING login details and then makes the transfer himself. That IS covered. It does NOT say a payment you send yourself is covered. Drop the \"contested reading\" framing.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c28"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U83: Fedwire-type wires are excluded from Reg E (12 CFR 1005.3(c)(3)) and governed by UCC 4A. Once accepted, a payment order cannot be cancelled unless the receiving bank agrees or a system rule allows it. (The \"bound under agency law\" clause is UCC 4A-202, which was not read; drop it.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c29"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U84: Under FedNow (12 CFR 210.44), the beneficiary's bank must pay the beneficiary immediately after accepting. Its only pause is when it has reasonable cause to believe the beneficiary is not entitled, in which case it may tell its Reserve Bank it needs more time.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c30"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U85: FTC 2025: bank payments carried the highest aggregate losses, then cryptocurrency. Credit cards were the most frequently reported method. The passage gives no dollar totals by method.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c31"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U86: IC3's crypto-investment pattern: contact by text, social media, ads or dating app; the victim is moved to a messaging platform; then introduced to \"investment groups\" posing as industry insiders (crypto or gold). Next come fake profits, loans to invest more, then \"taxes and fees\" at withdrawal, then recovery scams. IC3 says these scams are largely run from Southeast Asia using trafficked forced labor, and caused $7.2B in reported 2025 losses. (\"Investment clubs\" is IC3's separate stock-scheme section; don't merge the two.)","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c32"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U87: Reg E excludes transfers by a person the consumer gave the access device to. That exclusion does NOT apply when the device or credentials were obtained from the consumer by fraud (Comment 2(m)-3).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c33"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U88: The FBI's verification step is to hang up, look up the organization's contact details yourself and call that number. No outcome measurement is given.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c34"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U89: A freeze stops anyone, including you, from opening new credit. It is free, must be placed at all three bureaus and lasts until lifted. (That it doesn't help when you send money yourself is our inference from the mechanism; the FTC page doesn't say so.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c35"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U90: FBI victim guidance: contact your bank immediately and ask for a recall plus indemnification documents. Bank policies vary. File at ic3.gov whatever the amount, with full transaction details.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c36"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U91: FTC by-method steps: for Zelle or bank transfers, payment apps, wire companies and cards, report immediately and ask for a reversal. For gift cards, call the issuer on the number on the back and keep the card and receipt. For crypto, contact the exchange or ATM operator. For cash by mail, call USPIS at 1-877-876-2455 (it can intercept an undelivered domestic package for a fee). Then report at ReportFraud.ftc.gov.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c37"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U92: The Do Not Call Registry doesn't block calls and won't stop scammers. It only tells law-abiding telemarketers which numbers not to call.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c38"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U93: \"Your bank must refund you\" is false for payments you send yourself. If you were tricked into sharing login details or a code and the scammer then moved the money, the CFPB says that transfer IS unauthorized under Reg E.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c39"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"U104: The FTC says resist pressure and stop and talk to someone you trust. It names a family emergency as a common scam story. The page has no effectiveness data.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c40"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"G1: NEW, gap 1. A randomized trial on a representative US panel (Burke, Kieffer, Mottola, Perez-Arce; JEBO 2022) found that short video or text lessons made people less willing to invest in fraudulent pitches. The effect was gone at six months unless they got a reminder at three months. Legitimate investing was not reduced. It measures stated willingness, not money lost.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c41"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"G2: NEW, gap 1. A field experiment (Scheibe et al. 2014, N=895 past mail-fraud victims from USPIS lists) found that a warning phone call reduced acceptance of a mock telemarketing scam pitched 2 or 4 weeks later. The same-scam warning lost effect over time.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c42"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"G3: NEW, gap 1. A quasi-experiment on a Danish bank's mass warning message to clients over 40 (difference-in-differences and regression discontinuity) found no significant effect on fraud.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c43"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"G4: NEW, gap 1 (bank safeguards). An online experiment inside a mock UK banking app (CeDEx 2023 working paper) found that risk-based warnings with a clear cancel/postpone button cut falling for fraud by 81% versus control. Ordinary behavioural warnings cut it by 18%, and that effect faded as fraud repeated. It was a lab test, not live bank data.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c44"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"G5: NEW, gap 2. Credit cards: liability for unauthorized use is capped at $50 (Reg Z 1026.12(b)). Separately, a charge for goods or services \"not delivered as agreed\" is a billing error you can dispute in writing within 60 days of the first statement showing it (1026.13). Card-network chargeback rules are private and were not read.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c45"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"G6: NEW, gap 2. Reg E excludes Fedwire-type wire transfers.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c46"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"G7: NEW, gap 2. Frontier is explicit that its evaluation is theory-based, with no experimental counterfactual.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c47"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The FTC (October 2026 report to Congress) says older adults describe scammers keeping them on the phone for long stretches, sending them to the bank, a gift-card store or a Bitcoin ATM, coaching what to say to bank staff, and telling them not to talk to friends or family. This is the regulator's own summary of the isolation-and-coaching playbook.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"For gift-card scams specifically (FTC, December 2021; label \"in 2021\"), victims are kept on the line the whole time so they can't call anyone, and coached on what to tell a cashier who asks questions. The FTC calls store staff \"often the only people who can help\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The FTC's own list of the four signs: they pretend to be someone you trust, say there's a problem or prize, pressure you to act now (including telling you not to hang up so you can't check their story), and tell you how to pay. The same page's advice: stop and talk to someone you trust. (Page first published 2020-11-09; read 2026-11-07.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d3"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The FBI's \"Phantom Hacker\" warning (IC3 PSA, 2023-09-29) describes a staged, layered impersonation (tech support, then \"your bank's fraud department\", then a \"government\" agent) built to increase trust, and an instruction to keep the real reason secret. Almost 50% of tech-support victims Jan–Jun 2023 were 60+, with 66% of losses.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d4"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"FinCEN (Treasury's anti-money-laundering bureau) told banks in June 2022 what the playbook looks like at the counter. One red flag it lists is a customer taking directions from someone on a cell phone and unwilling to hang up. Another is buying large numbers of gift cards. It also describes the method as trust, then pressure through emotion or fear.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d5"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The method in FinCEN's words: impersonate someone trusted, then create high pressure through emotion or fear; gift cards are a growing payment request (FIN-2022-A002, 2022-06-15).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d6"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The FTC (August 2025) says the biggest current scams turn careful people's caution against them: fake security alerts exploit older adults' vigilance about protecting their money. This answers \"why smart people\": the pitch rewards being careful.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d7"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The Senate Aging Committee's chair (Collins, hearing of 2020-01-29) described the isolation step: the scammer cuts the victim off so no one can warn them, keeping them on the phone for hours or days and citing a \"confidential investigation\". (A senator's opening statement in the hearing record, not a study.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d8"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"\"Playbook\" is the researchers' word too. A 2006 study for the NASD (now FINRA) Investor Education Foundation analysed hundreds of undercover recordings of fraud pitches and counted over 1,100 uses of influence tactics (authority, fear, friendship, scarcity and others). Pitches were tailored to the target's psychology. This was investment and lottery fraud, 2006.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d9"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"FINRA Foundation, BBB Institute and Stanford (Sept 2019) surveyed 1,408 people (US and Canada) who had reported a scam to BBB Scam Tracker. 47% didn't engage, 30% engaged without losing money, and 23% lost money. Losing money was linked to the scammer seeming official, time pressure, feeling intimidated and fear of punishment (all p<.01). The sample is self-selected reporters and skews older, female and college-educated.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d10"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Same 2019 study: isolation measured. People with no one to discuss the offer with were more likely to lose money. Victims scored higher on loneliness (4.5 vs 4.0 on a 3–9 scale, p<.001).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d11"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Is \"smart people fall for it\" supported? For the mostly imposter and tech-support scams in the 2019 sample, education made little or no difference. A 5-question financial-literacy quiz gave a modest edge: 3.3 correct for those who hung up at once, 3.0 for those who engaged, 2.7 for those who lost money. The authors note that earlier investment-fraud studies found the reverse (more literate people lost more), and that scam type matters.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d12"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The source of \"smart people fall for it\": the 2006 NASD/FINRA study (165 verified investment and lottery fraud victims vs 150 random non-victims) found investment-fraud victims scored higher on financial literacy and were more educated, with higher incomes. Lottery victims were the opposite: older, more often living alone, less educated. So it is true for investment fraud, not for scams in general.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d13"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"AARP Foundation (March 2011) compared 723 law-enforcement-verified victims with 1,509 general-population adults. Investment and business-opportunity victims were more likely to have some college. Lottery and prescription-drug/identity-theft victims were less likely. It also found 37% of victims 55+ acknowledged being defrauded, against 56% under 55.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d14"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"In a cohort of 639 older adults without dementia (Rush Memory and Aging Project; mean age 82, mean 15 years of schooling), self-rated scam susceptibility went with lower cognition, lower well-being and lower literacy. Years of education were not associated after adjustment. It measured a 5-item susceptibility questionnaire, not actual losses (James, Boyle, Bennett, 2014).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d15"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Low scam awareness in 935 dementia-free older adults predicted later Alzheimer's dementia (HR 1.56 per 1-unit worse score) and mild cognitive impairment (HR 1.47), even after adjusting for cognition (Boyle et al., Annals of Internal Medicine, 2019). Rush's press release said \"twice the risk\"; the paper reports a per-unit hazard ratio and warns the measure is too weak to predict for any one person. Say \"a possible early sign\", never \"a test\".","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d16"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"CANADA, not US. In 151 Ontario adults aged 60–90, the 51 who reported being defrauded rated their own cognitive ability lower than non-victims (d=0.65). There were no demographic differences, and trust did not differ (Judges et al., Frontiers in Psychology, 2017). Cognitive ability was self-reported (MASQ), not tested.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d17"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Emotion beats knowledge in the lab. Kircanski et al. (Psychology and Aging, 2018; 139 people aged 65–85 and 30–40) induced excitement or anger, which raised stated intention to buy falsely advertised items. ⚠ The peer-reviewed abstract says the effect did NOT differ significantly by age (among people whose arousal actually rose). FINRA's summary brief says it held for older adults only, because it analysed the younger group separately. Don't say \"only older people\".","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d18"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The closest lab echo of \"I felt something wasn't right, but did it anyway\": in that study, older adults' intention to buy was not tied to how believable they found the ad. It tracked their emotional state instead. (Lab ads, not phone scams; FINRA Foundation brief.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d19"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The government's own review of the evidence (FTC Scams Against Older Adults Advisory Group, research committee with DOJ, CFPB and FINRA, April 2024) names four problems. People don't think it will happen to them; scammers raise emotions to disrupt thinking; warnings are forgotten; and even in-the-moment warnings often fail. It found \"relatively little research\" directly testing scam messaging. It also cites a lab finding that a heightened emotional state can stop people recalling earlier warnings during the scam (Daiku et al., 2020; that paper was not opened).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d20"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"A warning call shortly before the pitch roughly halves the yes rate, but no more. In AARP's experiments for DOJ (2003, \"Off the Hook\"), 75% accepted a sting offer with no warning and 37% after a peer counselor's warning call (Experiment 1, N=119; a three-day gap per Scheibe 2014). Professional con-artist callers got 92% of unwarned people to say yes.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d21"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Warnings often turn a confident yes into a doubtful yes rather than a no. In Scheibe et al. (2014; 511 past fraud victims reached by a mock scam 2 or 4 weeks after a warning call), warnings cut \"unequivocal acceptance\" but raised \"skeptical acceptance\", meaning saying yes while voicing doubt. The same-scam warning's effect was gone by 4 weeks. This is the research shape of \"felt something wasn't right, and went ahead\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d22"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Third parties stepping in: in the 2019 FINRA/BBB/Stanford survey, 20% of those who engaged said an organization intervened or tried to (bank tellers, wire-service staff, store checkouts). 51% of those who had an intervention avoided losing money. Self-reported, with no comparison group, so it doesn't prove the intervention caused the save. The same page notes scammers' scripts are designed to defeat exactly this.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d23"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Interviews with 27 gift-card scam victims (DeLiema, Volker and Worley, Victims & Offenders, 2023) found \"dissociative\" states under stress and self-blame afterwards. A cashier asking questions worked less well than a cashier refusing the sale. The FTC review (D20) adds that these victims saw the store's warning signs but had been coached to ignore them. Abstract only; full text paywalled and not read.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d24"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Why the cashier often doesn't stop it: Senate Aging testimony (2022-09-22) by the researcher behind D24 on her AARP-supported store study. Fewer than half of the cashiers surveyed had any purchase limit, managers had no formal training on what to say, and staff felt they could not refuse a customer who insisted. (Testimony; \"the our\" is in the transcript.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d25"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"In an AARP/AmeriSpeak survey of 2,179 US adults (Jan–Feb 2022), about 1 in 4 who bought gift cards to pay a supposed debt were warned by a store employee. Younger adults reported being targeted more (39% of 18–49 vs 28% of 50+). This is AARP's article about its own survey; the full survey report was not opened.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d26"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"No study found that tests a third person interrupting a call already in progress (a relative, teller or cashier), or a \"pause and talk to someone\" step, with a control group and a money outcome. The closest are D23 (self-reported, no controls), D24 (27 interviews) and D21–D22 (warnings given before the call). The FTC review lists \"can campaigns that encourage people to postpone decisions\" help as an open research question. Searched FINRA Foundation, AARP, FTC advisory-group and PubMed sources in this pass.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d27"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"A sworn public record of the whole playbook from the inside (Senate Aging Committee, written testimony, 2020-01-29). A Utah grandmother got a call from fake Social Security and DEA officials. She noticed something odd, checked the name online (it was real), and was told to act normal and tell no one. She was coached to tell the teller the money was for \"electronics\" and kept on speakerphone in her purse inside the credit union. She sent $118,464 by wire (about $150,000 lost in all). Not gift cards. She is named in the record; the episode uses her role only.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d28"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Same testimony: the moments where someone could have broken it. She asked to FaceTime the caller (refused) and then decided to trust him. A friend asked outright whether she was being scammed and she said no. Looking it up online alone broke the spell. Her own reflection on the teller:","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d29"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"NEWS (Portland Press Herald, 2019-04-12), a gift-card version. A 76-year-old Maine woman was kept on the phone for 8 hours and sent to five stores. Several stores tried to tell her it was a scam and she didn't believe them. She lost $1,000 in gift cards; a shipping company's policy stopped the remaining $4,400 in cash; friends convinced her that night. Named in the article; the episode does not name her.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d30"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The \"smart person\" and the aftermath, in sworn testimony (Senate Aging, 2022-09-22). A 76-year-old retired nurse educator who prided herself on \"sharp financial instincts\" was coached to tell her bank she knew the payee, and wired $20,000 out of fear. Afterwards she hid it from her sons and blamed herself. Wire, not gift cards. Named in the record; role only here.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d31"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Why scammers want gift cards, per the FTC (December 2021): easy to buy, few buyer protections, fast, largely irreversible and anonymous. Scammers name the brand and the store and send people to several stores.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d32"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The age skew, dated. In 2023, people 60+ reported $118 million lost through gift cards to the FTC, and gift cards were their second most frequent payment method (FTC advisory group open letter; undated, citing 2023 data). All ages in 2024: 41,120 reports, $212 million (Data Book 2024, row GC20).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d33"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"In 2025, older adults' gift-card payments were overwhelmingly on impersonation scams. Gift cards were their most frequent payment method on tech-support, romance, family-impersonation and prize scams (FTC, October 2026).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d34"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"What the industry was asked to do: the FTC advisory group's gift-card best practices (with Blackhawk Network) ask sellers for signage, cashier training on unusual purchase behaviour, and point-of-sale technical controls. They set no dollar cap. The FTC's 2025 report says Blackhawk led the work.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d35"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The Senior Safe Act (2018, 12 U.S.C. 3423) gives legal immunity for reporting suspected exploitation of a person 65+ to a regulator, police or Adult Protective Services. It protects the report only.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e1"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"\"Senior\" under the Act means 65 or older. (FinCEN's advisory uses 60+, so \"older adult\" differs by source.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e2"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The personal immunity covers supervisors and compliance or legal staff, not a frontline teller acting alone. The teller's role is to escalate.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e3"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The training condition: the BANK is immune only if every covered employee was trained before the report, and that includes staff who deal with seniors as part of the job (tellers). The training itself is optional (\"may provide\").","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e4"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The Senior Safe Act does not shield a bank for holding or refusing a transaction. Anything other than the report is left out of the immunity.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e5"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"In 2013, eight federal agencies said that federal privacy law (GLBA) generally does NOT bar a bank from reporting suspected elder financial abuse to the authorities.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e6"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"FinCEN's elder-exploitation advisory (FIN-2022-A002, June 15, 2022; the first was in 2011) asks institutions to detect and report, and to tag their SARs with a key term. It does not authorize holds.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e7"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"FinCEN's Financial Trend Analysis (April 2024): 155,415 elder-exploitation reports from June 15, 2022 to June 15, 2023, covering more than $27 billion in suspicious activity. The total includes attempted transactions, so it is not \"lost\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e8"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"In that FinCEN data, scams by strangers made up about 80% of elder-exploitation filings, with a median reported amount of $33,499 per scam filing (the average is $129,483, skewed by large cases). Use the median on air.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e9"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The Interagency Statement on Elder Financial Exploitation (Dec. 4, 2024; Fed, CFPB, FDIC, FinCEN, NCUA, OCC and state regulators) is a list of practices banks MAY adopt. It sets no requirement. ⚠ The text was revised in June 2026 to remove references to reputational risk.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e10"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"What the statement says banks may do about holds: they may use them only where the law allows, and the legal authority it points to is STATE hold laws.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e11"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The other side of the risk: the same statement's footnote warns that regulators have penalized banks for restricting customers' access to their accounts (Green Dot 2024, U.S. Bank 2023). Those cases were not about scam holds, but they show the regulatory risk of blocking a customer's money.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e12"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Trusted contacts at banks are optional (\"may establish\"). No federal rule requires a bank to ask a customer for one.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e13"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Handing customers material is ENCOURAGED by regulators and never required. This is the regulatory gap behind a teller having nothing to hand a customer.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e14"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Brokerage firms (not banks) have a federal hold rule. FINRA Rule 2165 lets a firm hold a disbursement from an account of someone 65+ (or impaired) for up to 15 business days, extendable by 10, then by 30 more if a regulator or court is notified. It is permissive and does not require the hold. In effect since Feb. 2018.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e15"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Brokerage firms must make reasonable efforts to collect a trusted contact (FINRA Rule 4512). Banks have no such duty, which is the contrast with E13.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e16"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"About half of US states have a \"hold\" law that lets a BANK delay a payment when it suspects exploitation of an older or vulnerable adult. This is the count the primary gives. The hand pass's own count from the FTC chart's list of states without a law is 24 states with one as of Oct. 2024. That number is derived, and it is pre-2025, so laws passed since may change it.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-e17"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"States with NO bank hold law as of Oct. 2024 include California, New York, Illinois, Pennsylvania, New Jersey, Georgia, North Carolina and Ohio: 26 states plus DC. Name a state on air only after re-checking it (the chart says it was a \"best effort\" and is not legal advice).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e18"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Examples of bank hold laws (FTC chart, Oct. 2024). Texas (Fin. Code §281): a hold is allowed for 65+ or disabled customers once the bank has filed a report, for 10 business days, and police can extend it by 30. Florida (Fla. Stat. §415.10341): 15 business days, plus up to 30 more. The chart summarizes both; the statutes were not opened.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e19"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Is there a GENERAL right for a bank to refuse an adult customer's own withdrawal because it suspects a scam? No federal statute or rule that grants one was found. The federal guidance points only to state hold laws (E11). Under the UCC, a bank that wrongly dishonors a customer's item is liable for damages. (Not checked: individual deposit-account agreements, which may give banks contractual discretion.)","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-e20"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The industry's own words on the liability fear, from a California banker (no hold law; the governor vetoed a hold bill in 2024).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e21"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Even where a hold law exists, only HALF of the surveyed banks had ever used it (ABA Foundation survey of 158 banks, Sept.–Oct. 2024). This is a direct measure of \"the room exists and goes unused\". ⚠ The survey is small and self-reported; the \"used it\" base is about 42 to 84 banks.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e22"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Why it goes unused, part 1: the blowback tellers describe is real and measured. More than 45% of banks that used holds reported negative customer reactions, and about 17% said customers closed their accounts after a delay (about 17% also reported positive reactions).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e23"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Why it goes unused, part 2: legal fear even where a law exists. About 60% of banks in hold-law states felt their immunity was enough, and 40% were uncertain or worried.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e24"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Does a hold work? Only the banks' own view exists: about 43% of banks that used one said it helped prevent exploitation, 33% said it was too early to tell, and about 10% said no. There are no loss figures.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-e25"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"\"Banks give customers nothing\" is PARTLY TRUE. The industry does run national consumer campaigns. #BanksNeverAskThat (an anti-phishing campaign) launched in Oct. 2020 with nearly 1,500 banks and had more than 2,000 by 2023. They are opt-in social-media and branch campaigns, not a handout duty at the counter. (The 2020 launch count comes from a separate saved ABA Banking Journal article.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e26"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The ABA Foundation's Safe Banking for Seniors program is free and includes training bankers and customer education. More than 2,050 banks have used it over 10 years (ABA, May 4, 2026). For scale, there are 4,212 FDIC-insured institutions today (FDIC API, Nov. 6, 2026). The comparison is loose: 2,050 is a cumulative count over a decade of mergers, so it is not \"half of banks use it now\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-e27"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The best US evidence that teller training works is AARP's BankSafe trial (2019, with Virginia Tech). Branches from 82 institutions were RANDOMLY assigned. Trained staff stopped $900,915 from leaving accounts, against $54,384 for the control group (16×). ⚠ One $500,000 case drives much of it; without it the gap is about 7×. The control group logged only 25 incidents, the savings are self-reported, and AARP runs the program.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e28"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Design check for E28, from the full report: randomized by branch; the outlier and the roughly 7× figure.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e29"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"How often banks STOP it, in the CFPB's SAR study (Feb. 2019; 2013–2017 data). In only 15% of elder-exploitation SARs was nothing lost (the transactions were blocked, rescinded or refunded). Older adults lost money in about 75%, with an average loss of $34,200. Fewer than one-third of SARs showed the bank also reported to police or APS.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e30"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"There is no later CFPB analysis with a \"stopped\" rate. The 2021 data spotlight counts 62,014 elder-exploitation SARs in 2020 but gives no loss-versus-stopped split. No 2022 or 2024 CFPB SAR analysis was found (one search).","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-e31"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"UK comparison (UK only): under the Banking Protocol, branch staff call police in real time. In 2025 it prevented £58.8M from being stolen, with 12,034 emergency calls and 136 arrests. Since it began in 2016: more than 81,000 calls, 1,662 arrests and £433M prevented. \"Prevented\" is the industry's own count, not an independent evaluation.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e32"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"FCC 26-67 (effective Nov. 25, 2026), bank part. Banks could already send fraud-alert calls and texts to a cell phone without prior consent, but only to the number the customer gave them. They may now also use a number from a \"reliable source\": a spouse or family member authorized on the account, the number the customer calls from, or another bank's records.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e33"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The limits that stay in place: banks must still honor opt-outs, and the cap on the number of exempt calls and messages remains. The change grants the ABA's 2015 petition. It is an alert-reach rule; it gives banks no power to hold money.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-e34"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"No US law found that caps how much in gift cards a store may sell one person. The state laws read in full or in synopsis (MD 2024, NJ 2025, NY 2023) require a warning notice, employee training, packaging and records; none sets a purchase limit or a hold. Searched the MD chapter text and the NJ S3587 text for \"limit\", \"$\" amounts and \"refuse\": the only dollar figure in MD's gift-card section is a $500 threshold for police evidence requests. NJ's own 2022 guidance lists a purchase limit only as a voluntary \"best practice\" (GC11). CONFIRMED-absence for the laws read; other states' bills not read.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-gc1"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Retailer caps are voluntary and came from pressure, not law. In Nov 2018 the New York and Pennsylvania attorneys general announced that Best Buy, Walmart and Target had cut the amount of store-branded gift cards a customer can buy in one transaction and the most that can be loaded on one card. The release does not give the new dollar figures.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-gc2"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The same 2018 release names the exact problem at the register: scammers coach victims to lie to the clerk who questions a big gift-card purchase, and once the codes are used, money is very unlikely to come back.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-gc3"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Best Buy publishes a cap: $500 per Best Buy card and a \"good faith\" effort to hold daily sales to $2,000 per person (Best Buy-branded cards; page undated, read 2026-11-09). \"Efforts\" is not a hard block.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-gc4"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Target publishes a limit only on Visa/Mastercard/Amex gift cards: 5 per guest per day, sold in $25–$200 amounts (so at most about $1,000 a day of those cards; that sum is our arithmetic). No published Target limit was found for Target GiftCards or for third-party brand cards (Apple, Google Play, etc.). A \"$200 limit\" sometimes cited may be a per-card size like this, not a purchase cap: an inference, UNRESOLVED. Page undated.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-gc5"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Walmart and the grocery chains: no published purchase cap found. Walmart's gift-card terms page blocked the fetch (bot check); Kroger's fraud page returned 403; Safeway's gift-card page carries fraud advice but no limit. Limits for these chains circulate only on deal blogs and forums, which the research rules bar. UNRESOLVED; the honest line is \"we couldn't find a published limit\", not \"there is none\".","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-gc6"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"A 2022 secret-shopper study (AARP-sponsored, 23 stores in one large US city) found signs were common but limits were not: nearly 80% had warning signs, fewer than half capped the amount, and the stores' measures would have limited purchases in about 22% of gift-card frauds and fully stopped about 9%. A conference abstract (Innovation in Aging supplement, Dec 2022), not a full paper.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-gc7"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"New York (first, 2023): since June 20, 2023 every business selling gift cards must post a scam warning at or near the display or register; the state publishes a model sign. Notice only, no cap and no training rule.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-gc8"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Maryland (Gift Card Scams Prevention Act of 2024, SB 760, Chapter 463): approved by the governor 5/09/2024, effective June 1 and Oct 1, 2025. Requires a fraud notice, training for all employees on spotting and responding to gift-card fraud, secure packaging, and 3-year records for third-party resellers. (A law-firm summary dates the signing Sept 24, 2024; the legislature's own page says 5/09/2024, so CORRECTED.)","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-gc9"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"New Jersey: training already required under a 2020 law (A802; staff trained by Aug 1, 2022 or within 45 days of hire). S3587 (enacted 2025, P.L.2025 c.39 per a news report) adds a state-worded notice at the gift-card display or register, in effect Oct 1, 2025; enforcement of the packaging rule was delayed to Feb 1, 2026 (news report, Nov 15, 2025).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-gc10"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"New Jersey's state guidance (Apr 2022) is the only official text found that tells stores to cap gift-card sales, and only as a voluntary \"best practice\", with keeping sales at staffed registers rather than self-checkout.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-gc11"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The national picture (NCSL, Feb 25, 2026): six states have merchant rules (DE, MD, NE, NJ, NY, RI), mostly a posted notice; only MD and NJ add training, packaging and records. In 2025, 22 states introduced at least 30 gift-card bills; Nebraska's LB 609 was the only new merchant-rule law that year (plus NJ's S3587).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-gc12"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Most of the 2025 wave is about card tampering (\"draining\"), not scam victims at the register: ten states (per the shopping-centre trade group ICSC) passed criminal-penalty laws modelled on Ohio's 2024 law. These punish thieves; they do nothing at the till for someone being talked through a purchase on the phone. Trade-group count; NCSL counts six in that category.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-gc13"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Pending: Pennsylvania SB1124 (introduced Dec 2025) would require standard fraud warnings and basic employee training where third-party gift cards are sold. Status after Dec 22, 2025 not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-gc14"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Federal: no bill found that sets rules for stores. The SWIPES Act (Sen. Moody, announced Aug 4, 2026) raises penalties and widens federal fraud and money-laundering law to cover gift cards; the saved text is an unnumbered draft. One tracker lists it as S. 5229 (not verified; congress.gov refused the fetch). Status beyond introduction: UNRESOLVED.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-gc15"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The FTC's role is advice to the industry, not rules. Under the Stop Senior Scams Act (law since Mar 15, 2022), an FTC-run advisory group published voluntary best practices (PDF dated Apr 2025): signs, cashier training, point-of-sale checks for scam-like purchases, and issuer checks at redemption. Nothing binding.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-gc16"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The FTC also gives retailers a free \"Stop Gift Card Scams\" kit (Dec 2020): rack signs, a register sticker, and a cashier card to put on the counter.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-gc17"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Does a cashier warning work? Only indirect evidence. AARP (2022) found 1 in 4 people buying gift cards in a scam had been warned by a store employee, and cites research that more than half of people who had a third party step in avoided losing money. AARP's \"tens of millions\" saved is a projection, not a measurement. No study found that measures the effect of register signs or cashier prompts on losses (searched: signage/cashier intervention effect studies; the RGCA's own site returned nothing).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-gc18"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"AARP's retail report (Mar 2022) says training alone isn't enough: the store must also let the cashier refuse a suspicious sale.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-gc19"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Size, all ages (FTC Data Books): gift and reload cards: 2023, 41,632 reports, $217M (read from the chart image, page 11; the extracted text lists the dollar values out of order); 2024, 41,120 reports, $212M. 2025 full year: not found (no 2025 Data Book on ftc.gov); NCSL cites FTC data for Jan–Sep 2025: over 30,000 reports, $199M. Reported losses only.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-gc20"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Age skew: people 60+ reported $139M lost by gift card in 2025 (+12% on 2024), and gift cards were 12% of their loss reports (read from the Figure 7 image). The FTC says gift cards were the payment older adults named most often in tech-support, romance, family/friend-impersonation and prize scams. (2023 figure for 60+, $118M, is from the FTC advisory group's letter, row D33.) No 2025 median gift-card loss for older adults found in the report.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-gc21"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Scammers name the brand and the store (FTC, data to Sept 2021, label \"in 2021\"): Target cards lost more than twice any other brand, with Target stores mentioned most, then Walmart, Best Buy, CVS and Walgreens. Target's median loss was $2,500; the all-brand median rose from $700 (2018) to $1,000. No newer FTC brand breakdown found. The FTC's current page (updated Jan 28, 2026) adds that scammers send victims to several stores so cashiers won't notice.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-gc22"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Recovery: the FTC says report to the card company right away, keep the card and receipt, and ask for a refund; Apple and Google can freeze money still on the card. \"Might give your money back\" is the FTC's own hedge. No recovery-rate figure found from the FTC, an issuer or a regulator. Page updated Jan 28, 2026.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-gc23"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The best measured freeze is one retailer's, years ago (news): Walmart's system flagged about 10,600 suspicious gift-card loads worth $4.4M and froze them in July 2017; about $4M went back to victims via a Secret Service forfeiture (CNBC, Apr 4, 2022, citing a Secret Service affidavit). News report of a court record; the affidavit itself not read.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-gc24"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"A grocery cashier who stopped it (news, KUTV Utah, Jan 9, 2024): a senior already on the phone with a fake \"PayPal\" agent was told to buy four $500 gift cards at a Smith's grocery store; the cashier saw the signs and refused the sale. Smith's says its cashiers are trained to start a conversation. Useful as the \"people around them\" example: a cashier who refused.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-gc25"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The family hears it first, and often they are the only ones who hear it. In the US government's own victim survey (BJS, 2017 data, published April 2021, all adults), about 1 in 7 fraud victims (14%) reported to police, 77% told family or friends, and 36% told ONLY family or friends.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af1"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Older victims, same survey: NIJ's reanalysis for people 60+ (NIJ Journal issue 286, 2024) found 19% reported to police and 84% told someone (a person or group, which includes family and friends, a bank, an agency or a lawyer). The 84% is not a family-only figure.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af2"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Reports to the authorities are a sliver. The FTC's own estimate of reporting rates (report to Congress, October 2026): matching nine of its law-enforcement cases to complaints gave 2.0% reporting where average losses were under $1,000 and 6.7% where they were over $1,000. Survey studies put reporting to a government body or the BBB at 4.8%. The FTC says most victims tell no one but family and friends.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af3"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Why people don't report (FINRA Investor Education Foundation, March 2015; online survey of 600 self-identified victims of investment-type fraud, August 2014, not representative): \"wouldn't have made a difference\" 48%, wanted to put it behind them 35%, embarrassed 29%, didn't know where to turn 26%. 68% told friends or family; 35% reported to authorities.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af4"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Self-blame is the norm, not the exception: 47% of victims blame themselves and 61% say they were \"too trusting\" (same FINRA 2015 survey, same caveats). The report found no demographic differences in agreeing with these statements.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af5"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The cost isn't only money: 65% reported at least one serious non-financial cost; severe stress 50%, trouble sleeping 38%, depression 35% (FINRA 2015, self-reported, self-selected sample).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af6"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Older victims in a nationally representative sample (60+, 2017 data, NIJ 2024): 27% had severe emotional distress, 31% moderate, 29% mild; only 11.7% none.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af7"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"⚠ A wrong figure in circulation: AARP (2025-03-11, \"Scams' Devastating Impact on Victims' Families\") says \"One in 5 scam victims age 60 and older report relationship problems with friends or family\", citing NIJ (March 2024). NIJ's own figure is 1 in 20 (4.6%). Its \"1 in 5\" is the share who reported to police. Never air \"1 in 5\". Also untraced: AARP 2023 (\"3 Ways You Can Help a Loved One Affected by a Scam\") says \"Just 52 percent of older scam victims told family or friends\" and \"one in three Americans think scam victims are largely responsible\", citing DeLiema's study and \"AARP survey data\". Neither primary was opened, and the 52% doesn't fit NIJ's 84% \"told someone\" (a wider category). Don't use them without the primary.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-af8"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"UK only. The UK Home Office's survey of people who reported to Action Fraud (published 2025-01-14) found 56% of fraud victims felt embarrassment, shame or self-blame, and about 1 in 10 had been hit by another fraud or cyber crime since their report. This is the only measured re-victimization rate found, and it isn't US.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af9"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Health effects: no study found that measures health or mortality effects of a STRANGER scam loss on older victims beyond self-reported distress (AF6, AF7). A news line that exploited elders are \"three times more likely to die\" traces to research on elder mistreatment more broadly; not opened, don't use. The measured link runs the other way: in a national panel (HRS, Lichtenberg et al., Clinical Gerontologist 2013), older adults with the most depressive symptoms and the least-met social needs had 3× the fraud rate. That's vulnerability, not aftermath, but it is the evidence that isolation matters.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-af10"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Recovery scams are built on lists of past victims. The FTC (page dated 2023-12-14) says scammers buy and trade \"sucker lists\" holding names, the scam that worked and the amount paid, then call back promising to recover the money for an upfront fee. No government agency charges to get your money back.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af11"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The FBI warns (PSA I-072026-PSA, 2026-07-20) that scammers impersonate FBI/IC3 staff to \"revictimize\" people who lost money. They use fake Facebook profiles and AI-generated videos of \"a senior FBI leader\" pointing to a spoofed IC3 site, and in one pattern they reach the victim right after the victim threatens to report. IC3 never contacts people by phone, email or social media and never charges to recover funds. An earlier PSA (I-041825-PSA, 2025-04-18) counted \"more than 100 reports\" of IC3 impersonation from December 2023 to February 2025. That is a count of reports, not a rate.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af12"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Repeat victimization rises with age (DeLiema, Langton et al., J. Elder Abuse & Neglect 2024; scammers' own records of 1.33M Americans who paid MAIL scams over 20 years): victims in their 70s and 80s were 9% more likely to be hit again than victims in their 50s. This is a relative risk for mail fraud only. No US absolute rate for repeat phone or text victims was found (searched: repeat victimization rate, recovery scam rate; FTC, IC3, NIJ).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af13"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The FTC's guidance for the helper (consumer alert, 2024-04-10, \"If someone you care about paid a scammer, here's how to help\"): lead with empathy, let them tell the story, validate it (the scammer alone is at fault), then \"ask what we can do next together\". EXPERT ADVICE ONLY: no evaluation of this guidance was found.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af14"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"National Elder Fraud Hotline, 833-FRAUD-11 (833-372-8311), run by DOJ's Office for Victims of Crime since March 2020, for adults 60+. Each caller gets a case manager who helps report the crime and connects them to resources. Open Monday to Friday, 10 a.m. to 6 p.m. ET, with English, Spanish and other languages. Over 121,000 calls so far, about 83 a day (the OVC page carries no updated date; read 2026-11-09). No published outcome evaluation found.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af15"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"AARP Fraud Watch Network Helpline, 877-908-3360, Monday to Friday 8 a.m. to 8 p.m. ET, free, for anyone, not only members (read 2026-11-09). It explicitly guides people whose loved one was targeted, but it does not investigate or recover money.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af16"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"AARP's free online Fraud Victim Support Group (with Volunteers of America, \"ReST\"): confidential groups of about one hour, led by trained facilitators, as many sessions as you want, and family members and friends are explicitly welcome. No outcome evaluation found (searched: ReST evaluation, outcomes).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af17"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"No controlled evaluation of any fraud-victim support service was found: not the US hotline, not AARP's groups, not the no-blame conversation guidance. The closest thing is UK only: the Home Office survey (2025-01-14) found about 60% of victims satisfied with the support they got, driven by how fast and how often they were contacted and by honesty about whether the case would be investigated. Satisfaction is not an outcome.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af18"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Gift cards (FTC, page dated 2026-01-28): call the card company, report it no matter how long ago, keep the card and the store receipt, and ask for the money back. Apple and Google say they can FREEZE money still on the card. The FTC promises nothing: \"Some companies… might give your money back.\" Then report at ReportFraud.ftc.gov.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af19"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The FBI's own elder-fraud checklist (IC3, read 2026-11-09): call the bank, and for a wire ask for a recall AND a \"hold harmless letter\"; protect credit with the three bureaus; report to local police and ic3.gov (IdentityTheft.gov if identity was stolen); the Elder Fraud Hotline can help file the IC3 report. It adds the hold-harmless letter and the local police report to F21 (row C7).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af20"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"⚠ Adult Protective Services may NOT take a stranger-scam report. In states whose law requires the abuser to be in \"a position of trust\" (a son, a caregiver), a scam by a stranger usually doesn't qualify (NCEA brief hosted by ACL, revised August 2021). APS might still open a \"self-neglect\" case if the loss leaves the person unable to afford groceries or medication. APS help is voluntary: a competent adult can decline it (ACL fact sheet). Rules differ by state.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af21"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"A little-known aftermath step, taxes: an IRS Chief Counsel memo (CCA 202511015, released 2025-03-14) says losses from scams where the victim moved money with a profit or \"safeguard and reinvest\" motive are deductible theft losses. Its examples are a fake bank \"fraud specialist\" who had the victim move retirement money to \"protect\" it, a pig-butchering investment and a phishing impersonation. Romance and fake-kidnapping losses are NOT deductible for 2018–2025. Not tax advice; the memo is non-precedential, and whether the same treatment holds for 2026 losses was NOT checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af22"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The measured case for stepping in. In a 2019 survey of 1,408 people who reported a scam attempt to BBB Scam Tracker (Stanford Center on Longevity, FTC staff, FINRA Foundation, BBB Institute), 20% of those who engaged said an organization tried to stop it (bank tellers, wire-service staff). Of people who reported a third-party intervention, 51% avoided losing money. It is a survey with no control group: it doesn't show what share would have stopped anyway.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af23"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Scammers plan for the helper: the same report says scripts are built to beat third-party intervention, telling the target to speak to no one and coaching them on what to say to the cashier or teller. That's why \"just ask them\" at the counter often fails, and why a family member's call may land differently.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af24"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Having someone to talk to mattered: those who didn't discuss the offer with anyone while it was happening, or had no one to discuss it with, were more likely to lose money. Losing money went with more loneliness (association, not proof of cause).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af25"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Trained bank staff stop more (AARP BankSafe pilot with Virginia Tech, release 2019-10-17; six months, 1,800+ employees, ~500 branches, 11 states): trained staff kept $900,915 from leaving accounts versus $54,384 for untrained staff (16×), or $865 vs $70 per employee (12×), and reported over four times as many suspected cases. AARP evaluated its own program, the savings are self-reported, and the release doesn't say how staff were assigned (the full report does: randomized by branch, row E29).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af26"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"UK only, the counter-to-police model: the UK's Banking Protocol trains branch and Post Office staff to call police (a 999 call) when a customer seems to be mid-scam. In 2025 it made 12,034 emergency calls, led to 136 arrests and \"prevented\" £58.8M from being stolen; since it began, 81,000+ calls, 1,662 arrests and £433M (UK Finance Annual Fraud Report 2026, June 2026). \"Prevented\" is the industry's own count with no counterfactual. No US equivalent was found.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af27"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Trusted contacts: US bank regulators (interagency statement, 2024-12-04: Fed, FDIC, OCC, NCUA, CFPB, FinCEN, state regulators) say banks MAY let customers name a trusted contact to call when exploitation is suspected. It's voluntary for the bank, and the contact can't see the account or move money. Brokerages must ASK for one, and naming one is optional for the customer (SEC/FINRA/NASAA investor bulletin, no page date). Some state laws let banks hold a suspicious transaction. The bank-powers detail is in Part E; the family step is \"ask to be named\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af28"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Secrecy is part of the script, in the FTC's own words: in the family-emergency and gift-card scams the caller says to send money now \"but not tell anyone\" (FTC, 2026-01-28). Australia's regulator describes the isolation step for long scams: once trusted, the scammer tries to cut the target off from family and friends (Scamwatch, 2025-11-24).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af29"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"Written helper resources do exist in the US, but they are scattered blog-length pieces: FTC's \"Pass It On\" (feature page first published 2014-04-17: \"Share What You Know. Stop Scams.\") plus its 2024-04-10 alert (AF14); AARP's \"3 Ways You Can Help a Loved One Affected by a Scam\" (2023-06-30) and \"Scams' Devastating Impact on Victims' Families\" (2025-03-11, which calls family members \"secondary victims\"); AARP's helpline and support groups (AF16, AF17); the CFPB/FDIC \"Money Smart for Older Adults\" curriculum (last enhanced July 2021); a nonprofit sheet (Give an Hour, 2024-09-12). NCOA and state-AG guides were not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-af30"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The fullest government treatment found is Australian: Scamwatch has a whole \"Help someone who's being scammed\" section with a red-flags checklist and conversation starters for family (2025-11-24). No single US government page of that scope was found in this pass. So \"the helper angle is rare\" holds for a full US treatment; it does not hold for \"nobody has written about it\". The video side was checked separately.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-af31"},{"s":"ep55-scam-calls-and-texts","n":55,"c":"The FTC's gift-card page (2026-01-28): only scammers tell you to buy a gift card and give them the numbers; no real business or government agency will ever tell you to buy a gift card to pay them. Aired as the episode's one rule (x05plan.c).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-s1"}]);